    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many three-digit numbers have the property that their digits are in strictly increasing order, when read from left to right?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{9}{3}=84$",
          "worked": "Zero cannot be included: it would have to be the first digit. Choose three digits from $1,2,\\ldots,9$; each choice has exactly one increasing arrangement.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\binom{9}{3}\\\\\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\frac{9\\times8\\times7}{3\\times2\\times1}\\\\\\text{Number of numbers}&=84\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "How many three-digit numbers have the property that their digits are in strictly decreasing order, when read from left to right?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{10}{3}=120$",
          "worked": "Choose three distinct digits from $0,1,\\ldots,9$. Each choice has exactly one decreasing arrangement, whose first digit is nonzero.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\binom{10}{3}\\\\\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\frac{10\\times9\\times8}{3\\times2\\times1}\\\\\\text{Number of numbers}&=120\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "How many $n$-digit numbers consist of strictly increasing digits, when read from left to right?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{9}{n}$ for $1\\le n\\le9$; $0$ for $n>9$.\n\nChoose distinct nonzero digits; their increasing order is fixed.",
          "worked": "Zero cannot occur because it would be the leading digit. Choose $n$ distinct digits from $1,2,\\ldots,9$. Each selection gives exactly one increasing arrangement, so the number is $\\binom{9}{n}$ for $1\\le n\\le9$. For $n>9$, there are no such numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q16-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "How many $n$-digit numbers consist of non-decreasing digits, when read from left to right?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{n+8}{8}$.\n\nUse eight dividers to allocate the $n$ digits among $1,2,\\ldots,9$.",
          "worked": "Zero cannot occur: in non-decreasing order it would be a leading digit. Repetition is allowed among the digits $1,2,\\ldots,9$.\nRepresent the $n$ digits by $n$ identical marks separated into nine groups by eight dividers. Empty groups are allowed. The groups give the numbers of $1$s, $2$s, through $9$s, and the digit order is then fixed.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\binom{n+8}{8}\\\\\\text{Number of numbers}&=\\frac{(n+8)!}{8!n!}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
